# SYNOPSIS

```text
send [-i from] [-s source] snapshot target
```

# DESCRIPTION

send copies a stream of a Taisce volume's snapshot (taisce(4)) from fs(4)'s `send` to another fs's `receive/`<sys>, which applies it to its volume's `recv/`<sys>.
<snapshot> and <from> are snapshots' names, as `snap` lists them: `2026/1030`.
Without `-i`, the stream is all of <snapshot>; with it, only what changed from <from> to <snapshot>, which the receiver must hold already, as the last stream it took.
<source> is the `send` to read, `/svc/fs/send` by default, this node's.
<target> is a `receive/`<sys> file, most often a member's through 9gate(4): its `file` role exports its fs tree, so `/svc/9gate/n/`<node>`/receive/`<sys> once imported.

The stream is the snapshot's nodes, not its blocks: each file and directory that differs, its names, kind, mode, time and attributes, and only those of its blocks whose checksums differ.
So an incremental after a small change is small, whatever the volume's size.
The receiver applies it with ordinary changes, so its own indices and journal follow, and its own key encrypts it where it has one; between nodes the stream is sealed by 9gate's Noise IK.
Each node received keeps the sender's volume and inode in its `sys:sent-ino` attribute, which the receiver indexes; `recv/`<sys> keeps the sender's volume in `sys:sent-from` and the snapshot it now holds in `sys:sent-txg`.
Once a stream is applied, the receiver takes a snapshot of its own, so it keeps each one received.

send says how many bytes it sent.

# EXAMPLES

Send this node's volume to node2, which keeps it at `recv/node1`, then each day's changes:

```rc
echo import tcp!node2!564 node2 >/svc/9gate/ctl
send 2026/1029 /svc/9gate/n/node2/receive/node1
send -i 2026/1029 2026/1030 /svc/9gate/n/node2/receive/node1
cat /svc/9gate/n/node2/status
```

